  "body": "Do we need a uniform way of signaling to a stream that the consumer has lost interest? For example, If you open a stream to read a file, but subsequently decide to stop reading, should there be a way to signal you intend to consume from the stream no longer, and the OS should close the underlying file descriptor?\n\nNode.js does not have this in its base stream interface, but it does have various ad-hoc mechanisms inside various stream implementations, e.g. the undocumented `fs.ReadStream.prototype.destroy` or `http.IncomingMessage.prototype.destroy`.\n\nWhat happens if you do this but you have multiple consumers? E.g. does handing out a readable stream to Alice and Bob give Bob the ability to call `stream.abort()` and make Alice no longer able to read? Or do you employ some sort of reference-counting mechanism to ensure all consumers must call abort?\n\nSpeaking of reference counting, would weak-reference semantics suffice? I.e. once no more references to a stream exist in the program, automatically abort it (calling the creator-supplied abort mechanism)? This seems promising. It could be made to work with pipe if internal references were juggled appropriately, e.g. piping retains a reference until the writable stream errors or similar.\n\n/cc @Raynos @creationix @dominictarr who in their alternative stream implementations for Node.js have baked in an abort primitive.\n",
